    <h2>The Myth: LVP Fixes Everything</h2>

    <p>The pitch is seductive — waterproof, scratch-resistant, cheaper than hardwood, installs fast, looks like wood. So it gets recommended for every room and every problem, and homeowners come to believe LVP is simply the best floor, full stop. It is not a best floor; it is a right-for-certain-jobs floor. Treating it as the universal answer is how people end up covering a solid oak floor with plastic, or getting a bouncy, gapping install because "it just clicks together" made prep sound optional. The material is good. The one-size-fits-all belief around it is the problem.</p>

    <h2>The Reality: The Subfloor Decides</h2>

    <p>Whatever goes on top, the floor's success is decided by what's underneath. Floating LVP demands a subfloor flat to within about 3/16 inch over 10 feet — lay it over an unflat, dirty, or damp base and it telegraphs every hump, flexes underfoot, and separates at the seams within a couple of years. On a concrete slab, moisture has to be tested and managed before anything is glued or floated, or vapor pushes up and lifts the floor. Here is what goes wrong on the rushed installs: the crew skips the leveling and the moisture check because the customer was told LVP is foolproof, and the "foolproof" floor fails in a way that is entirely about the prep nobody did.</p>

    <h2>What Good Installers Do That the Cheap Ones Skip</h2>

    <p>The real work in flooring is the hour before the pretty part. Good installers flatten the subfloor with leveling compound or sanding, clean it to bare, moisture-test a slab, and only then lay the floor. And on the older homes near the historic district — many of which have original solid hardwood under decades of carpet — the good installer's most valuable move is telling you not to cover it. Solid hardwood can be sanded and refinished five to seven times over its life; refinishing it usually costs less than a new floor and restores real character and resale value that a plank of vinyl over the top erases forever. That is where flooring overlaps with a <a href="/locations/hartselle/general-contractor/">general contractor</a> on a bigger remodel and with a <a href="/locations/hartselle/painter/">painter</a> whose trim-and-finish work has to meet the new floor cleanly.</p>

    <h2>Red Flags in a Flooring Quote</h2>

    <p>The warning signs are quiet. A quote with no line for subfloor prep or leveling — because a flat subfloor is assumed, not verified. A slab job with no mention of moisture testing. An installer who reaches for LVP over your existing hardwood without ever raising refinishing as an option, which means they are selling the easy install, not the right floor. The honest admission a good installer offers: sometimes the floor you already have, restored, beats anything you'd buy — and sometimes LVP genuinely is the smart, durable choice for how your family lives. A pro will tell you which, even when the more profitable answer for them is the other one.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Flooring Costs in Hartselle</h2>

    <p>Real ranges:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Luxury vinyl plank</strong> — about $3 to $7 per square foot installed, depending on product and subfloor prep.</li>
      <li><strong>Refinishing existing hardwood</strong> — roughly $3 to $5 per square foot, often cheaper than replacement and restoring more value.</li>
      <li><strong>New solid hardwood</strong> — around $8 to $14 per square foot installed.</li>
      <li><strong>Subfloor prep</strong> — leveling, moisture control, and old-floor removal, the line cheap bids leave off.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The number to watch is prep. Two LVP quotes can differ because one includes flattening a wavy subfloor and the other is planning to lay planks over the waves and hope.</p>

    <h2>The Smart Play</h2>

    <p>Match the material to the room, not the trend: LVP where water and heavy traffic rule, hardwood or refinished hardwood where warmth and value matter, and always ask the installer to walk you through subfloor prep before you sign. If your home is one of the older ones and you suspect hardwood under the carpet, have someone look before you buy new flooring — you may already own the best floor in the house. There is no seasonal urgency to flooring, which means you have time to get the material choice and the prep right rather than rushing either.</p>

    <h2>Questions Hartselle Homeowners Ask</h2>

    <p><strong>Should I put LVP everywhere?</strong></p>
    <p>Great in kitchens, baths, and busy family rooms. A poor choice as a cover over original hardwood or an unprepped subfloor. Match it to the room.</p>

    <p><strong>Why is my new vinyl floor uneven or gapping?</strong></p>
    <p>The subfloor. LVP needs it flat to about 3/16 inch over 10 feet and, on slabs, moisture-tested. The plank telegraphs whatever it's laid over.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>LVP $3 to $7 a square foot installed; refinishing hardwood $3 to $5; new solid hardwood $8 to $14.</p>

